good morning everyone  today oh my  good morning everyone my name is juliet  morrison and i'm an assistant professor  at university of california riverside  and today i'll be speaking to you about  how lung immune populations  can drive influenza outcomes i will also  demonstrate how  one can actually use gene expression  data bioinformatic tools  and statistical analyses to generate  novel hypotheses  about immune cell involvement in viral  diseases  so for a little background viral disease  severity is determined not only by the  virus but also the host responds to that  virus so it's a very delicate  balancing act which means therefore  that too much of our response is bad  and too little of a response is bad you  have to have a delicate balance  one of the goals of my research is  developing host targeted viral  therapeutics  so what i mean by this is that if you  can imagine that  viruses versus hosts are like children  on a seesaw  in this scenario here we have the host  response  being stronger than the viruses but at  any moment we could have  additional viruses coming in and  overwhelming that host response  if there's too much of a host response  however you will also get tissue damage  and disease  today i'll be talking about seasonal  influenza  it results in three to five million  cases of severe disease and  two hundred and fifty thousand to five  thousand deaths annually  and it causes a great economic burden  about  11.2 billion in economic costs per year  in the us are related to influenza  infections  so i was interested in finding out if  there could be  any signatures that could distinguish  influenza severity  based on their pathogenicity and  serotype across a wide variety of  influenza viruses  and so to do this we combined two  different studies  a very similar design where in one study  we have mice being infected with  different um influenza viruses some  avian and one human  and in the second study we have a  variety of  h1n1 human viruses  and then we take these  data we take this and then we  harvest the lungs from these mice and we  took out  we look at the viral titers and we look  at histopathology and we do  transcriptomics  and additionally we are also taking  weight loss and survival data  once we combine these data  we then integrate everything to come up  with a  larger um a larger  experimental we then combine these data  to come up with a  larger experimental data set  and i want to point out in the  left hand corner i'm showing you a  weight loss curve  of the different viruses in this  particular  compendium we are we have a variety of  viruses that  lead to severe weight loss and death in  mice  so the um sad face with the um crossed  out eyes  we have viruses that can lead to some  weight loss but then the animals recover  and then we have other viruses that  cause very mild  um disease and so the but the mice do  not lose  weight in this situation and so we did  the transcriptomics on the lungs of  these different mice  and we found that we got  we we found that there were increases in  the inflammatory response  in the animals that had more severe  disease  we also found quite strikingly that  there was this  decreasing coagulation and lipid  metabolism  gene expression in the animals that  developed severe disease  and instead we saw more we saw an up  regulation of certain lipid metabolism  and coagulation genes in the mice that  developed mild disease and survived  and this we found we we have highlighted  here in the black box  so what does this even mean well why  are we seeing these transcriptional  differences well  the first thing to consider is that the  lung is an amalgam of different cell  types so we have multiple epithelial  cells we have different immune cell  populations  and we wanted to find out which of these  populations  was causing these differences in  transcriptional responses that we were  seeing  and to interrogate this we used a tool  called digital cell quantifier or dcq  and what dcq does is that it takes an  immune cell compendium from imgen  with many different immune cell types  and then it combines it with  your own gene non-white gene expression  data so  our lung transcriptional data and  then they infer the in viva global  dynamic  changes in the immune cell quantities  within your lung  tissue so basically dcq can predict the  temporal  process of an immune cell response to  stimuli  within a mouse model  after we got the results of the dcq  we then used um statistical analysis to  correlate the presence of these  different populations  from imgen with the weight loss that we  found in these animals  and you can see here  you can see here that we see that  a prediction of lung disease  being decreased as the disease  intensity increases you also see that  pcq predicts um that granulocytes will  increase in the long in more severe  disease  and we also see that alveolar  macrophages are predicted to decrease  in severe disease and monocytes are  predicted to  increase in severe disease and these are  things that we already know  and i'm showing you here how we  validated  some of these results so we take  our mice and we infect them with either  halo9 which is  a mild flu or with pr8 to  give a severe disease or we use pbs and  then we harvest the lungs  at day three and we look at the  different immune populations that were  predicted by dcq  we found that our data matched what bcq  predicted  in that we found that cd11c positive  signal  f positive cells aka alveolar  macrophages  were highly abundant in pbs  infected or pbs controlled mice um  were decreased in our california o9  infected mice  and severely decreased in our pr8  infected mice  we also found that the granulocytes were  lower in quantity in pbs  um treated mice and were  much higher in our pr8 infected mice  as has been shown previously and finally  we also showed  that the lye 6c positive monocytes  are greatly enriched in the lungs of  our pr8 infected mice so  with more severity we see more monocytes  thus alveolar macrophages monocytes and  other immune cell types  correlate with the disease severity  we're discussing here  now i also want to bring your attention  to this population here in green  this is the small puritano peritoneal  macrophage population  so what would peritoneal macrophages  even be doing in the lung  well it turns out that the peritoneal  cavity which surrounds our abdominal  organs  is closely related to two other cerosal  cavities  the epi epicardium as well as the pleura  so the pleural cavity is the  serosal cavity that surrounds our lungs  and in fact the cells of the pleural and  peritoneal cavities are quite similar  both the pleural cavity and the  peritoneal cavity  contain macrophages such as  the small macrophages and the large  macrophages and they're distinguished  by their size as well as their the  markers upon their surfaces  so how do we sort plural macrophages  well in my group we take a live cells  single cells and then we sort  for cells that are cd115 positive and  cd11b positive  and then within that population we  separate them based on  mhc class 2 positivity or f480  positivity  so small plural macrophages are mhc  class 2 positive  and f480 low while the large plural  macrophages  are f480 high and mhc  class 2 low  now for some  real experiments so we wanted to ask  whether  this population that resembles our  peritoneal macrophages  are actually plural macrophages and so  to do this we  we took a very simple approach it's  actually very hard to  um target the pleural cavity but we have  actually developed a procedure in the  morrison lab  where we can introduce dyes and other  things into the pleural cavity without  damaging the lung  so in this experiment we are doing an  intrapleural  injection of a macrophage i call pkh  and when you harvest the pleural fluid  you can see that  we have these red cells here because the  macrophages have  specifically taken up this dye  now if we do that same injection of the  pleural cavity with our pkh and then  follow up with  an intranasal infection with our  with mild or severe flu strains  or pbs we find that  after we remove the lungs and then look  at them under the microscope  we find that we don't see any red cells  in our  pbs treated control mice  but we see a great deal of  red cells aka the plural macrophages  in the lungs of our california o9  infected  mice and there are much lower numbers of  these macrophages in our pr8  infected mice so these are the mice that  get severe flu  and die  and this actually supports our initial  prediction  because we had predicted that in the  case  of severe flu that we wouldn't be seeing  this plural macrophage population  and we further followed up by looking at  the histopathology  of these mice and found that  in the case of our california 09 versus  our pr8 we see a lot more debris  and bronchiolor collapse in our pr8  mice showing that this is indeed severe  flu  versus mild flu  so what is the phenotype of these  trafficked macrophages  well we went ahead and lo did flow  cytometry on  these populations and we found that our  pkh  positive macrophages were actually  actually looked a lot like alveolar  macrophages so cyglic  f positive and cd 11b um  low and we also found strikingly that  the trends the the macrophages that had  accumulated in the lung were also murty  k  negative as opposed to the general  murtik positive macrophages  we're not quite sure what that means yet  but it's an interesting observation  and now if we take the flow cytometry  data and  graph it you can see more strikingly  that  the pleural cavity in the pleural cavity  with the  addition of our viruses we see a  decrease  in the numbers of pleural macrophages  and in the lung we see this increase  of that of the pleural macrophage  populations and specif  especially with our california o9 so we  see an accumulation  of macrophages that originally  originated from the pleural cavity that  and they have now accumulated in the  lung  in the case of mild flu but they do not  accumulate  in the case of severe flu  we then asked if plural macrophages  could have an  impact on influenza outcomes does the  fact that they move  from the pleural cavity into the lung  actually  impact our outcomes  so to do this we injected the pleural  cavity of mice with either  quadrant liposomes or with pbs  liposomes and these hydrogenate  liposomes when taken up by macrophages  will induce apoptosis in the macrophages  so we're basically clearing out the  pleural macrophages  from the pleural cavity and when we do  this  we actually see very targeted  um macrophage depletion because if you  look at the  pleural macrophage population  both the small pleural macrophages and  the large pleural macrophage is  are decreased in the pleural cavity  but we don't see any impact on our lung  macrophage populations  or the populations of the draining lymph  nodes  so plural targeting via with with  padrina  is very very specific for plural  macrophages  so with that in hand we went ahead and  we  did an experiment where we do  intrapleural injections of the quadrant  liposomes versus the  our control liposomes and then a day  later we infect with our mild  um flu strain the california o9  and then we weighed the mice over the  course of five days  and we found that in the case of the  mice that had their pleural macrophages  depleted  they lost weight more quickly than the  mice that  had the control liposomes implying that  the plural macrophages do serve a  protective function  in influenza  we also looked at the viral titers in  the  lung tissue and we found them to be um  pretty comparable for both the quadrant  and the pbsl  untreated mice suggesting that it's not  necessarily a  viral um a difference in the viral  tighter that's driving this and  more most definitely a host response  and um finally i'd like to summarize  what i've discussed today  which is that the plural cavity may  serve as a reservoir for lung  macrophages  and in this case we found that both  small plural macrophages and large  plural macrophages  appear to cross the mesothelium  and enter the lung which is a a very  new um idea furthermore  we know that they are having an impact  on  um influenza resolution and for  influenza outcomes  um how this is happening we don't know  it has actually opened up a much larger  many more questions because we don't  know what's driving the macrophages into  the lung  and we don't know what kind of cells  they're really becoming in the lung  and we also are trying to figure out the  mechanisms of  how it's actually alleviating the severe  disease  and finally i'd like to acknowledge  the members of my group and our  collaborators  who have been involved in this project  thank you so much for listening and have  a good day

